/// LoopbackReceiver — one-shot loopback callback listener for native apps
/// (RFC 8252 §7.3).
///
/// `start` binds an `HTTPServer` to `127.0.0.1` on an OS-assigned port.
/// The first `GET /callback` carrying the flow's `state` is recorded and
/// answered with a static "you can close this tab" page that echoes no
/// provider data. Any other path or method, any callback with a missing,
/// wrong or repeated `state` (or a repeated `code` or `error`), any
/// callback with neither `code` nor `error`, any callback whose `iss`
/// names another issuer than the one given to `expectIssuer:`, and any
/// later callback answers 404 without touching the recorded query.
/// Browsers fetch `/favicon.ico` right after the redirect, and any web
/// page or local process can fire a junk `/callback` at the port, so a
/// slot that took the first request would let either block the login.
///
/// ## The expected state
///
/// The receiver learns the flow's `state` from `expectState:`, which must
/// be called before the authorize URL is opened: the redirect can arrive
/// as soon as the browser has the URL, and a callback that arrives before
/// the receiver knows the state is refused (404), not buffered. A native
/// app creates the session, then hands its state over:
///
/// ```beamtalk
/// sess := OAuthSession newSessionFor: server client: reg
/// (store create: sess boundTo: rx binding) unwrap
/// rx expectState: sess state
/// url := (OAuthFlows authorizeUrl: server client: reg scopes: scopes session: sess) unwrap
/// query := (rx awaitCallback: 300000) unwrap
/// ```
///
/// It is a setter rather than `awaitCallback:state:` because the await
/// usually starts after the URL is opened, too late to filter a callback
/// that beats it. `awaitCallback:` without a prior `expectState:` answers
/// a `config` error at once instead of waiting out its timeout.
///
/// One receiver serves one flow: `expectState:` raises when a state is
/// already expected. A timeout reports how many callbacks were refused,
/// so a receiver armed with the wrong state (the binding, or another
/// session's state) shows up as such rather than as a user who never
/// signed in.
///
/// ## The expected issuer
///
/// `expectIssuer:` (normally `server issuer`) adds an RFC 9207 check: a
/// callback whose `iss` is present and names another issuer is refused
/// like a wrong state. A callback without `iss` is still accepted, unless
/// `expectIssuer:required:` made it required (for a provider that
/// advertises `authorization_response_iss_parameter_supported`), and
/// `OAuthFlows handleCallback:store:boundTo:` keeps its own `iss` check.
/// `OAuthFlows completeLoopbackLogin:...` sets it; call it yourself, before
/// opening the authorize URL, when composing the steps by hand. A
/// callback recorded before the issuer was known, and not yet handed out,
/// is checked again when it is set.
///
/// These checks only keep junk from using up the one-shot slot.
/// `OAuthFlows handleCallback:store:boundTo:` still checks the state
/// against the `SessionStore`, and that is what stops a forged code.
///
/// `state` is not a secret from the local machine, though. The
/// authorize URL carrying it can show up in the argv of the process that
/// opens the browser and in browser history, so a local process that
/// reads it can still send a matching junk callback (`code=junk`) first
/// and take the slot. It cannot get the forged code accepted (PKCE and
/// the session store stop that); the provider answers `invalid_grant`.
/// It cannot take the slot with a callback no provider would send (a
/// wrong `iss`, no `code`; BT-4613), only with a well-formed junk code.
/// So the receiver can be re-armed (BT-4572): `rearm` clears the slot and
/// keeps the same port and expected state, and
/// `awaitCallback:keepListening:` leaves the listener up after a callback
/// so there is something to re-arm. `OAuthFlows completeLoopbackLogin:...`
/// does both, and puts the session back, when the exchange answers
/// `invalid_grant`, up to a cap (3 by default) within its overall
/// timeout; prefer it to composing the steps by hand.
///
/// Residual limit: a local attacker that reads `state` can still delay
/// the login, by spending the re-arms with a flood of well-formed junk
/// codes or by waiting out the timeout, or end it, with a forged `error=`
/// callback (never retried, since it looks the same as
/// the provider's own) or a junk code the token endpoint refuses with an
/// error other than `invalid_grant`. A real redirect that lands while a junk callback
/// holds the slot, before the re-arm, answers 404 and is lost. None of
/// these can hijack the login. Web pages and blind port scans cannot use
/// up the slot at all.
///
/// The receiver owns the per-flow `binding` (`Secrets generate`), so a
/// native app passes `rx binding` to the session store and never makes
/// one itself.
///
/// `awaitCallback:` waits in the calling process, for a message from the
/// handler, not in an actor call: the user may take minutes, and actor
/// calls time out at 5 s. That is why the receiver is a `Value` handle
/// (its methods run in the caller) over two actors, the `HTTPServer` and
/// its `LoopbackGate` handler, which holds the one-shot state.
///
/// The listener stops once `awaitCallback:` answers, either way; `stop`
/// stops it early and is idempotent. `awaitCallback:keepListening:` with
/// `true` leaves it up after a callback, for `rearm`; the caller then
/// stops it.
///
/// ## Lifetime
///
/// The listener does not outlive the process that owns it. Until
/// `awaitCallback:` begins, the owner is the process that called `start`:
/// if it crashes (exits with any reason but `normal`), the listener stops.
/// A normal exit leaves it up, since the receiver may have been handed to
/// another process (or the REPL evaluation that started it has ended).
/// Once `awaitCallback:` begins, the waiting process is the owner, and
/// the listener stops if it exits for any reason before the wait answers.
/// It stays the owner after a wait that keeps the listener up, so a
/// process that crashes, or ends, between a callback and `rearm` still
/// takes the listener with it.
///
/// A receiver that nobody awaits stops after `defaultLifetime` (10
/// minutes), or the lifetime given to `startWithLifetime:`. The wait
/// itself is bounded only by its own timeout. A listener stopped by its
/// owner or its lifetime answers a `config` error from `awaitCallback:`
/// and raises from `expectState:`.
///
/// ## Examples
/// ```beamtalk
/// rx := LoopbackReceiver start unwrap
/// rx redirectUri                       // => "http://127.0.0.1:50278/callback"
/// rx binding size                      // => 43
/// rx expectState: sess state           // before opening the authorize URL
/// query := (rx awaitCallback: 300000) unwrap
/// query at: "code"                     // => "..."
/// ```
typed Value subclass: LoopbackReceiver
  /// The listening server.
  field: server :: HTTPServer | Nil = nil
  /// The actor behind the server's request handler, holding the one-shot
  /// callback state.
  field: gate :: LoopbackGate | Nil = nil
  /// The OS-assigned port the server listens on.
  field: port :: Integer = 0
  /// The per-flow session binding.
  field: binding :: String = ""

  /// How long, in ms, a receiver that is never awaited keeps listening:
  /// 10 minutes.
  class defaultLifetime -> Integer => 600000

  /// Start a listener on `127.0.0.1`, port 0 (OS-assigned), with a fresh
  /// binding, that stops after `defaultLifetime` unless `awaitCallback:`
  /// has begun by then. Answers a `transport` error when the listener
  /// cannot start.
  class start -> Result(LoopbackReceiver, OAuthError) =>
    self startWithLifetime: self defaultLifetime

  /// Like `start`, but the listener stops after `lifetimeMs` unless
  /// `awaitCallback:` has begun by then. Answers a `config` error when
  /// `lifetimeMs` is outside `1..maxTimeout`.
  class startWithLifetime: lifetimeMs :: Integer -> Result(LoopbackReceiver, OAuthError) =>
    ((lifetimeMs < 1) or: [lifetimeMs > self maxTimeout])
      ifTrue: [
        ^Result error: (OAuthError config: "LoopbackReceiver lifetime must be between 1 and {self maxTimeout} ms")
      ]
    binding := Secrets generate
    // The gate monitors this process from its own `initialize`, and
    // starts the server inside one of its own methods: a `DOWN` that
    // arrives meanwhile waits in its mailbox until the server is
    // attached, so a crash at any point here cannot leak the listener.
    gate := LoopbackGate spawnWith: #{#starter => Pid current}
    outcome := Result
      tryDo: [
        srv := gate listenWith: (self handlerFor: gate) lifetime: lifetimeMs
        self new: #{
          #server => srv,
          #gate => gate,
          #port => srv port,
          #binding => binding
        }
      ]
    outcome
      ifOk: [:rx | Result ok: rx]
      ifError: [:e |
        [gate close] on: Error do: [:ignored | nil]
        Result error: (OAuthError transport: "loopback listener failed to start: {e message}")
      ]

  /// The server's request handler: a block, so it runs in the connection
  /// process, the only one that can read the raw query string (to spot a
  /// repeated parameter, which `queryParams` hides). It forwards each
  /// request to `gate`, which decides. A failed read raises, so the server
  /// answers 500 and nothing is recorded: the check fails closed.
  class handlerFor: gate :: LoopbackGate -> Block =>
    [:req :: HTTPRequest |
      raw := req exchange isNil ifTrue: [nil] ifFalse: [req exchange query]
      gate handle: req rawQuery: raw
    ]

  /// The largest `awaitCallback:` timeout, in ms: the BEAM's limit for a
  /// receive timeout (about 49 days).
  class maxTimeout -> Integer => 4294967295

  /// Block the calling process until `{#beamtalk_oauth_callback, tag, query}`
  /// arrives from the gate process `gatePid`, `timeoutMs` passes
  /// (`#timeout`) or the gate exits (`#stopped`).
  class await: tag :: Reference from: gatePid :: Pid timeout: timeoutMs :: Integer -> Result(Dictionary, Object) =>
    // `{callback, Query}`, `timeout` or `stopped` from the native module.
    answer := Erlang beamtalk_oauth_loopback
      await: tag
      gate: gatePid
      timeout: timeoutMs
    (answer isKindOf: Tuple) ifTrue: [^Result ok: (answer at: 2)]
    Result error: answer

  /// The redirect URI to register with the provider for this flow:
  /// `http://127.0.0.1:{port}/callback`.
  redirectUri -> String => "http://127.0.0.1:{self.port}/callback"

  /// Record only a callback whose `state` is `expected`, normally
  /// `sess state`. Call it before opening the authorize URL: a callback
  /// that arrives earlier answers 404 and is lost. Answers the receiver.
  /// Raises when `expected` is nil or empty, when a state is already
  /// expected (start a new receiver for another flow), or when the
  /// receiver is stopped.
  expectState: expected :: String -> LoopbackReceiver =>
    self.gate expectState: expected
    self

  /// Refuse a callback whose `iss` is present and is not `expected`,
  /// normally `server issuer` (RFC 9207); a callback without `iss` is
  /// still accepted. Call it before opening the authorize URL, next to
  /// `expectState:`; a callback already recorded and not yet handed out
  /// is checked again. Answers the receiver. Raises when `expected` is nil
  /// or empty, when another issuer is already expected (the same one again
  /// is fine), or when the receiver is stopped.
  expectIssuer: expected :: String -> LoopbackReceiver =>
    self expectIssuer: expected required: false

  /// As `expectIssuer:`, but when `required` is true a callback without
  /// `iss` is refused too: pass true when the provider advertises
  /// `authorization_response_iss_parameter_supported` (RFC 9207 §3), as
  /// it then always sends `iss` and the code exchange refuses a callback
  /// without one. Once required, `iss` stays required.
  expectIssuer: expected :: String required: required :: Boolean -> LoopbackReceiver =>
    self.gate expectIssuer: expected required: required
    self

  /// Wait up to `timeoutMs` for the callback and answer its raw query
  /// (`code`, `state`, or `error` and `error_description`), for
  /// `OAuthFlows handleCallback:store:boundTo:`. A callback that arrived
  /// before the call is answered at once.
  ///
  /// The wait happens in the calling process, which blocks: call this from
  /// a plain or forked process, not from inside an actor method, or that
  /// actor stops answering (and its callers time out) while the user signs
  /// in. The listener is stopped before this answers. Errors:
  ///
  /// - `callback_timeout`: nothing arrived in time; its `detail` is the
  ///   number of callbacks refused for a missing, wrong or repeated
  ///   parameter (or no `code` or `error`, or a wrong `iss`), also given in the description when it is not 0;
  /// - `config`: a timeout outside `0..maxTimeout`; `expectState:` was
  ///   never called; the receiver was stopped (by `stop`, its owner
  ///   exiting or its lifetime passing), before or during the wait;
  ///   another process is already waiting on it; or it already answered
  ///   a callback with the listener kept up and has not been re-armed.
  ///   Only in the stopped case is the listener gone: otherwise it stays
  ///   up, and any other wait carries on.
  awaitCallback: timeoutMs :: Integer -> Result(Dictionary, OAuthError) =>
    self awaitCallback: timeoutMs keepListening: false

  /// As `awaitCallback:`, but when `keep` is true and a callback arrives
  /// the listener stays up, still owned by the calling process, so that
  /// `rearm` can wait for another callback on the same port. The caller
  /// must `stop` it when done. Every error still stops the listener as
  /// `awaitCallback:` does.
  awaitCallback: timeoutMs :: Integer keepListening: keep :: Boolean -> Result(Dictionary, OAuthError) =>
    ((timeoutMs < 0) or: [timeoutMs > self class maxTimeout])
      ifTrue: [
        ^Result error: (OAuthError config: "awaitCallback: timeout must be between 0 and {self class maxTimeout} ms")
      ]
    tag := Reference new
    registered := Result tryDo: [self.gate awaitFrom: Pid current tag: tag]
    (registered isError or: [registered value =:= #closed])
      ifTrue: [
        self stop
        ^Result error: (OAuthError config: "LoopbackReceiver is stopped")
      ]
    registered value =:= #noState
      ifTrue: [
        ^Result error: (OAuthError config: "call expectState: on the LoopbackReceiver before opening the authorize URL")
      ]
    registered value =:= #busy
      ifTrue: [
        ^Result error: (OAuthError config: "another process is already waiting on this LoopbackReceiver")
      ]
    registered value =:= #held
      ifTrue: [
        ^Result error: (OAuthError config: "this LoopbackReceiver already answered its callback; rearm it before waiting again")
      ]
    gatePid := self.gate pid
    outcome := self class
      await: tag
      from: gatePid
      timeout: timeoutMs
    (keep and: [outcome isOk]) ifTrue: [^Result ok: outcome value]
    refused := 0
    (outcome isError and: [
      outcome error =:= #timeout
    ]) ifTrue: [refused := [self.gate refusedCount] on: Error do: [:e | 0]]
    self stop
    // A callback that raced the timeout may already be in the mailbox:
    // take it, which also leaves no stray message behind.
    (outcome isError and: [outcome error =:= #timeout])
      ifTrue: [
        outcome := self class
          await: tag
          from: gatePid
          timeout: 0
      ]
    outcome
      ifOk: [:query | Result ok: query]
      ifError: [:reason |
        reason =:= #stopped
          ifTrue: [
            Result error: (OAuthError config: "LoopbackReceiver was stopped while waiting")
          ]
          ifFalse: [Result error: (OAuthError callbackTimeoutRefusing: refused)]
      ]

  /// Clear the one-shot slot after a callback from
  /// `awaitCallback:keepListening:`, so the next callback carrying the
  /// expected state is recorded. The port, the expected state and issuer,
  /// the binding and the owner (the process that waited) stay as they were.
  /// Answers the receiver, or a `config` error when the receiver is
  /// stopped (then it is stopped for good), when no state is expected,
  /// or while a wait is still pending.
  ///
  /// Use it only after the code from that callback was refused (the
  /// provider's `invalid_grant`), together with putting the session back
  /// (`SessionStore restore:boundTo:`); `OAuthFlows
  /// completeLoopbackLogin:...` does both.
  rearm -> Result(LoopbackReceiver, OAuthError) =>
    answer := Result tryDo: [self.gate rearm]
    (answer isError or: [answer value =:= #closed])
      ifTrue: [
        self stop
        ^Result error: (OAuthError config: "LoopbackReceiver is stopped")
      ]
    answer value =:= #noState
      ifTrue: [
        ^Result error: (OAuthError config: "call expectState: on the LoopbackReceiver before re-arming it")
      ]
    answer value =:= #busy
      ifTrue: [
        ^Result error: (OAuthError config: "a wait on this LoopbackReceiver is still pending")
      ]
    Result ok: self

  /// Stop the listener, then its handler. Idempotent: answers `#ok` every
  /// time, like `Actor>>stop`, and never raises, so the handler is stopped
  /// even when the server's shutdown fails. The server gets 10 s, more
  /// than the 5 s its own engine shutdown may take.
  stop -> Symbol =>
    self.server isNil ifFalse: [
      [self.server stopWith: #normal timeout: 10000] on: Error do: [:e | nil]
    ]
    self.gate isNil ifFalse: [[self.gate stop] on: Error do: [:e | nil]]
    #ok

  /// Whether the listener is still accepting connections.
  isListening -> Boolean => self.server notNil and: [self.server isAlive]

  /// `LoopbackReceiver(http://127.0.0.1:{port}/callback)`. The binding is
  /// not shown.
  printString -> String => "LoopbackReceiver({self redirectUri})"

  /// The JSON shape, with the binding redacted.
  asJson -> Dictionary =>
    #{"redirectUri" => self redirectUri, "binding" => OAuthError redacted}
